      "term": "Civilian Government",
      "matchText": "Civilian Government",
      "type": "concept",
      "definition": "Kim Young-sam's 1993–98 administration, dubbed the Munmin Jeongbu (“Civilian Government”) because he was the first elected president in three decades who had not come from the military — the label marked the formal end of army rule over South Korean politics."
    },
    {
      "term": "Namyeong-dong",
      "matchText": "Namyeong-dong",
      "type": "place",
      "definition": "The Seoul police interrogation center where the South Korean security services ran their most sensitive political investigations under the dictatorship; it was where the student Park Jong-chul was tortured to death in 1987. The building now houses a democracy-movement memorial."
    },
    {
      "term": "Hanbo",
      "matchText": "Hanbo",
      "type": "concept",
      "definition": "Hanbo Steel, a South Korean conglomerate whose collapse under enormous debt in early 1997 was the first major corporate failure of the crisis year — an early signal that the chaebol borrowing model had run past its limit."
    },
    {
      "term": "Sammi",
      "matchText": "Sammi",
      "type": "concept",
      "definition": "Sammi Group, a mid-sized South Korean chaebol centered on specialty steel that went bankrupt in 1997, one of the wave of conglomerate failures that preceded the full Asian Financial Crisis."
    },
    {
      "term": "Lee Jong-chang",
      "matchText": "Lee Jong-chang",
      "type": "people",
      "definition": "The Yonsei University student photographed cradling his mortally wounded classmate Lee Han-yeol after Lee was struck by a tear-gas canister on June 9, 1987. The image became the defining photograph of the June democracy protests."
    }
